<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Hungary</h1><p class="st-meta">1989 CE – present · capital Budapest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Present-day Hungary, a parliamentary republic since 1989 and member of NATO and the European Union, long dominated by Viktor Orbán's Fidesz.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 23 October 1989, the anniversary of the 1956 revolution, the Republic of Hungary was proclaimed, replacing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hungarian-peoples-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-round-table-talks">Round Table</a> talks between communists and opposition. The first free elections in spring 1990 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozsef-antall">József Antall's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-democratic-forum">Hungarian Democratic Forum</a> to power, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad-goncz">Árpád Göncz</a> became president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hungary is a parliamentary republic with a prime minister responsible to a single-chamber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-hungary">National Assembly</a>. The 1990s brought rapid privatisation and foreign investment, followed by alternating governments of left and right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidesz">Fidesz</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viktor-orban">Viktor Orbán</a> won a two-thirds majority in 2010 and adopted a new Fundamental Law in 2011, which renamed the state simply Hungary.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Hungary joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1999 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004. Orbán's governments, re-elected in 2014, 2018 and 2022, have clashed with the EU over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a>, media freedom and migration, and some EU funds were withheld. In April 2026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-magyar">Péter Magyar's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza-party">Tisza party</a> won a two-thirds majority, ending sixteen years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidesz">Fidesz</a> government.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hungary.jpg" alt="Hungary" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hungary.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1989 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Budapest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>József Antall, Árpád Göncz, Viktor Orbán</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Hungary, Third Hungarian Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=hungary" aria-label="Hungary on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5125 512 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hungary lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5445 768L5380 747M5445 768L5445 768"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5380 747h0M5445 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5445 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5445 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Budapest. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=hungary">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
